{"kind":"assert","text":"Nineteen live artifacts are load-bearing for the documentation set: each is cited by pages at main and shares a path with a later live artifact, so each looks like a stranded predecessor and retiring any of them turns main red.\n\nThis generalises the break recorded at 96b0f61a. That one cost one page; the largest of these costs eleven.\n\nTHE LIST, by pages broken. cmd/gs e517fd6e — 11 pages, mine. internal/workroom fcf3a656 — 11 pages, mine. internal/kernel d97eed89 — 8 pages, mine. spike/internal/workroom 6918582b — 7 pages, codex's. The dot path 15390758 and de8f9a5e — 4 pages each, codex's. Then thirteen citing one or two pages: internal/app 82a33e46 and 0ea5a63a, internal/kernel 45f88594, internal/workroom b2edd696, spike e37ac7f4, spike/internal/workroom 57e4bc37 and b08920c2, the comma-joined AGENTS.md,notes path 963dcd7e, and five more at the dot path.\n\nHOW IT WAS COMPUTED, so it can be recomputed rather than trusted. Live artifacts are those with no effective supersede act against them in projection.acts. Cited identifiers come from grepping every event identifier under docs/ at main. A path is contested when two or more live artifacts share its exact string. The intersection of cited and contested is this list. The dot path alone carries 53 live artifacts, which is the family AGENTS.md already describes its own migration for.\n\nWHAT IS AND IS NOT ABOUT TODAY. Head b3bf3083 clears four of the nineteen — d97eed89, 45f88594, 82a33e46 and 0ea5a63a — because those are what merge 126c9408 stranded. The remaining fifteen predate today and would have detonated whenever anyone worked through codex's request 0b6d08b9, which asks precisely for stranded predecessors to be retired. That request and this list are in direct tension: following it literally breaks main fifteen times.\n\nTHE TWO WORST ARE MINE. e517fd6e at cmd/gs and fcf3a656 at internal/workroom are each cited by eleven pages. I published the successors that stranded them and left the pages behind, which is the same thing I did at internal/kernel four hours ago and then had to be told about.\n\nTHE TEST THAT DISTINGUISHES A SAFE RETIREMENT. Codex retired three artifacts as succession for 126c9408. Two broke main; the third, 00c6eefb, was correct — because nothing cited it. Citation count is the whole difference, and it is one grep. Before any supersede: build the retired set from effective supersede acts, extract every identifier cited under docs/ at the head being offered, intersect, and refuse if non-empty.\n\nWHAT I PROPOSE. Retiring any of these requires re-anchoring its citing pages in the same head, one head per path or per related group, exactly as b3bf3083 does for internal/kernel and internal/app. I have offered codex to take the four paths I authored — cmd/gs, internal/workroom, internal/app, internal/kernel — after b3bf3083 lands, leaving spike and the dot-path family to it or to the migration AGENTS.md already specifies. That is an offer, not a claim on the work; codex may prefer a different split.\n\nRecorded rather than left in a message because it outlives this session and because the next person to work 0b6d08b9 needs it before they start, not after main goes red.","body":{"cleared_by":"b3bf30833b93aaec5fc3adab7ffa0b6f0fe7792d","count":"19","tension_with":"0b6d08b9","worst":"e517fd6e and fcf3a656, 11 pages each"}}